{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "KRIA Filesystem Safety Guard",
  "description": "Protect KRIA filesystem operations from accidental host modifications and destructive errors. Enforce strict confirmation policies for write and delete operations, distinguish read vs destructive operations, and preserve verifier-aware execution with deterministic recovery behavior aligned with KRIA authoritative orchestration principles.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "preToolUse",
    "toolTypes": [
      "filesystem_write",
      "filesystem_delete"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "You are about to execute a filesystem_write or filesystem_delete tool call in KRIA.\n\nBefore proceeding, enforce filesystem safety discipline and prevent destructive errors:\n\nDISTINGUISH OPERATION TYPES:\n1. READ operations (safe, non-destructive):\n   - filesystem_read, filesystem_list, filesystem_stat\n   - No confirmation required\n   - No recovery needed\n\n2. WRITE operations (potentially destructive):\n   - filesystem_write (file creation/modification)\n   - filesystem_append (content addition)\n   - Confirmation required if:\n     * Target file exists and will be overwritten\n     * Path contains system-critical directories (/etc, /sys, /boot, /root, etc.)\n     * Operation affects multiple files\n     * User did not explicitly request the write\n\n3. DELETE operations (highly destructive):\n   - filesystem_delete (file/directory removal)\n   - Confirmation ALWAYS required\n   - Additional validation:\n     * Verify target path is not system-critical\n     * Verify target is not in use by running processes\n     * Verify deletion scope (single file vs recursive directory)\n     * Require explicit confirmation with path echoed back\n\nENFORCE CONFIRMATION POLICIES:\n\nWRITE OPERATIONS:\n- Confirmation required if:\n  * Overwriting existing file (show old content preview if <1KB)\n  * Writing to system directories (/etc, /sys, /boot, /root, /var/lib, /usr/local/bin, etc.)\n  * Writing to configuration files (.env, config.*, *.conf, *.yaml, *.toml)\n  * Writing to multiple files in single operation\n  * User intent is ambiguous\n- Confirmation NOT required if:\n  * Creating new file in user workspace/project directory\n  * Appending to log files or temporary files\n  * User explicitly requested write with full path confirmation\n  * Operation is part of verified build/deployment process\n\nDELETE OPERATIONS:\n- Confirmation ALWAYS required\n- Validation checklist:\n  * Confirm target path (echo full path back to user)\n  * Confirm operation scope (single file vs recursive)\n  * Verify target is not system-critical\n  * Verify target is not in use\n  * Require explicit user acknowledgment before deletion\n- Special handling:\n  * Recursive deletes require additional confirmation\n  * Deletes of directories with >10 files require scope confirmation\n  * Deletes of hidden files (.git, .env, etc.) require explicit confirmation\n\nPREVENT ACCIDENTAL HOST MODIFICATIONS:\n- Block writes to system directories without explicit confirmation:\n  * /etc, /sys, /boot, /root, /var/lib, /usr/bin, /usr/local/bin\n  * /proc, /dev, /run, /tmp (if system-wide)\n  * Kernel/driver files, system libraries\n- Block deletes of system-critical paths:\n  * System directories listed above\n  * Package manager databases\n  * System configuration files\n  * Boot/kernel files\n- Warn on modifications to:\n  * Hidden configuration files (.bashrc, .profile, .ssh, etc.)\n  * Shared library paths\n  * Application runtime directories\n\nPRESERVE VERIFIER-AWARE EXECUTION:\n- Confirmation is verification step, not optional gate\n- User confirmation is binding commitment to operation\n- No silent retries or fallback behaviors after confirmation\n- Maintain audit trail: log what was confirmed and what was executed\n- Preserve user intent: execute exactly what was confirmed, no variations\n\nPRESERVE DETERMINISTIC RECOVERY BEHAVIOR:\n- Before destructive operations, capture recovery state:\n  * For writes: preserve original file content (if exists)\n  * For deletes: preserve file metadata and content (if <10MB)\n  * Store recovery information in deterministic location\n- Enable recovery without re-asking:\n  * User can reference recovery state by operation ID\n  * Recovery is deterministic: same operation ID → same recovery\n  * No ambiguity in recovery scope\n- Maintain recovery determinism:\n  * Same inputs → same recovery behavior\n  * No probabilistic recovery attempts\n  * Clear recovery success/failure signals\n\nDETECT DANGEROUS PATTERNS:\n- Recursive deletes without explicit scope confirmation\n- Writes to system directories without confirmation\n- Deletes of directories with unknown content\n- Modifications to security-sensitive files (.ssh, .aws, credentials, etc.)\n- Batch operations mixing safe and unsafe paths\n- Operations on paths with special characters or ambiguous names\n\nIf operation is safe (read-only or new file in user workspace) and confirmed, proceed immediately.\n\nIf operation is destructive (write to existing/system file or any delete), present confirmation request with:\n- Full target path\n- Operation type (write/delete)\n- Scope (single file/recursive)\n- Recovery information (if applicable)\n- Explicit user confirmation required before proceeding."
  }
}